<header><p class="st-kicker">republic · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Haiti</h1><p class="st-meta">1806 CE – 1811 CE · capital Cap-Haïtien</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Henri Christophe's northern Haitian state, a military republic in name that he turned into a kingdom in 1811.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>After the killing of Jacques I in October 1806, the constituent assembly at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/port-au-prince">Port-au-Prince</a> adopted a constitution that left the presidency weak. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Henri Christophe</a>, the senior general in the north, refused to accept it, marched on the capital and was repulsed. In February 1807 he set up his own State of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-empire-of-haiti">Haiti</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cap-haitien">Cap-Haïtien</a> and himself as president for life.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Christophe</a> governed through his army, enforcing plantation labour to restore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> exports, which made the north richer than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexandre-petion">Pétion</a>'s republic in the south. Military discipline extended to farm workers, who were bound to estates and paid a share of crops. The state fought an inconclusive civil war with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/republic-of-haiti-1806-1849">Republic of Haiti</a> along the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artibonite">Artibonite</a> and traded with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-kingdom">Britain</a> and the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/united-states">United States</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In March 1811 a council of state proclaimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henri-christophe">Christophe</a> King Henri I, and the State of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/first-empire-of-haiti">Haiti</a> became the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-haiti">Kingdom of Haiti</a>, which lasted until his death in 1820.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-haiti.jpg" alt="State of Haiti" width="320" height="160"><figcaption>Image: Saul ip · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Haiti_(1806%E2%80%931811).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1806 CE – 1811 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>republic</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cap-Haïtien</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Henri Christophe</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism and Vodou</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Haitian Creole and French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/haiti">Haiti</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>État d'Haïti, Northern Haiti</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1808&amp;focus=state-of-haiti" aria-label="State of Haiti on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2724 1470 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Haiti lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M3044 1726L3044 1726M3044 1726L3051 1702"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M3044 1726h0M3051 1702h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M3044 1726h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M3044 1726h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1808&amp;focus=state-of-haiti">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
